    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "makes sense. what are some of the more common \"hidden\" errors or edge cases that designers usually miss when they're doing these audits? like stuff that doesn't immediately show up in a contrast checker?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "A common oversight is assuming that just because the text passes a contrast check, the *interactive state* does too. For instance, the hover or active state of a button might have a lower contrast than the default state. Designers often forget that when a user is navigating with a keyboard or assistive technology, the visual feedback for focus needs to be as clear as the text itself. You should also check for insufficient touch target sizing—making buttons too small is a major accessibility failure, even if the text inside is perfectly readable.",
      "source": "target",
