    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "yeah that touch target thing is a good point. i've also heard people talk about how weird little bugs or glitches can pop up during testing that are super hard to track down. how do people usually deal with those kinds of elusive, annoying interface issues?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Elusive interface issues, or \"gnomes in the machine,\" are often the most frustrating to squash. They are usually rooted in how the code handles edge cases—like an unexpected input or a very specific device/OS combination. To tackle these, you need rigorous, multi-faceted testing, which means testing on a diverse range of real devices, not just the latest flagship models. Furthermore, logging detailed user journeys, including device specs and OS versions, is key to tracking down those sneaky bugs.",
